# Settings' "Memory" screen (App memory usage) reads /proc/pagetypeinfo.
#
# com.android.internal.app.procstats.ProcessStats.updateFragmentation()
# (frameworks/base/.../procstats/ProcessStats.java:689) opens the file directly
# to build the memory-fragmentation view. When Settings drives procstats for its
# own UI the parse happens in the SETTINGS process, i.e. in system_app -- not in
# system_server, which AOSP does grant this type.
#
# Measured on build 77, reproduced on demand by launching the screen:
#
#   avc: denied { read } for comm="ndroid.settings" name="pagetypeinfo"
#     scontext=u:r:system_app:s0 tcontext=u:object_r:proc_pagetypeinfo:s0
#     tclass=file permissive=0
#
# It fails SILENTLY: the reader is wrapped in catch (IOException), which clears
# mPageTypeNodes/Zones/Labels/Sizes, so the screen simply shows no fragmentation
# data and nothing logs an error. A denial with no visible failure is exactly the
# class BLUEPRINT section 9 warns about in the other direction -- worth grepping
# for the consumer before deciding it is noise.
#
# GRANTED rather than dontaudit'd, because upstream's own posture is that this
# data is fine for system-side domains and only off-limits to apps:
#
#   private/system_server.te:1126  system_server gets proc_pagetypeinfo r_file_perms
#   private/incidentd.te:29        incidentd likewise
#   public/system_app.te + private/system_app.te:155
#                                  "settings app reads /proc/version" -- the same
#                                  Settings-reads-a-/proc-node shape, already granted
#
# Neverallow checked first (BLUEPRINT section 9): private/app_neverallows.te:186
# forbids this type to `all_untrusted_apps`, and that macro (:5) is exactly
# { ephemeral_app isolated_app mediaprovider mediaprovider_app untrusted_app
#   untrusted_app_25/27/29/30 untrusted_app_all }. system_app is not a member, so
# the rule is legal rather than merely un-rejected.
#
# Both types are declared in system/sepolicy/public (file.te:55, system_app.te:7),
# so this belongs in sepolicy/private and NOT in sepolicy/vendor -- naming it from
# vendor policy would compile it as the versioned type proc_pagetypeinfo_33_0 and
# make it invisible to the obvious grep, which cost three false negatives on
# build 74.
#
# VERIFIED LIVE on build 77 before this was written, via `ksud sepolicy patch`:
#   before  2 records, reproduced on demand from android.settings.APP_MEMORY_USAGE
#   after   0 records, with 52 ndroid.settings log lines in the same capture as
#           the positive control, so the zero is a measured zero and not a
#           screen that never opened.
allow system_app proc_pagetypeinfo:file r_file_perms;
